/// Basecamp — Litestone Schema
///
/// THE SEED. Every other realm derives from this file. Nothing here is
/// hand-mirrored anywhere else: the SQL in `db/migrations/` is generated from
/// this schema, not written alongside it.
///
/// Data hierarchy:
///   Account → User → Workspace → { WorkspaceMember, Project, Server, Secret }
///                                     └ Project → Environment → App
///                                                               ├ Deployment → DeploymentStep
///                                                               └ Job → JobRun
///
/// Conventions (Litestone, not optional):
///   Model names are PascalCase singular. The table name is snake_case of the
///   model name (`WorkspaceMember` → `workspace_member`), the client accessor
///   is camelCase (`db.workspaceMember`).
///   Field names are emitted as columns VERBATIM — Litestone does NOT
///   snake_case them. `workspaceId` is the column, not `workspace_id`.
///   DateTime is stored as ISO-8601 TEXT, not epoch milliseconds.
///   Tables are STRICT by default.
///
/// ACCESS CONTROL — every model declares @@gate. Read the level, not the hooks.
///
/// The level is a fact about the caller IN A WORKSPACE, not about their row in
/// `user`: the same person is `owner` in one workspace and `viewer` in the next.
/// api/src/core/gate.ts is the mapping and the whole ladder; `membershipClaim()`
/// is what puts `memberRole` on the principal, once per request, off the
/// WorkspaceMember row for the workspace being addressed.
///
///   0 STRANGER   no session, or suspended
///   1 VISITOR    authenticated, no standing in any workspace — reads Workspace
///   2 READER     viewer / billing
///   4 USER       developer
///   5 ADMIN      admin
///   6 OWNER      owner
///   7 SYSADMIN   User.isSystemAdmin — the hub tier
///   8 SYSTEM     asSystem() only: jobs, outposts, seeds, migrations
///   9 LOCKED     nothing passes, asSystem() included
///
/// Reading the gates below: "2.4.4.5" is R.C.U.D — a viewer reads, a developer
/// creates and updates, an admin deletes. A missing position inherits the one
/// before it, so "5" is admin for all four and "2.5" is read-by-anyone,
/// written-by-admin. 8 in a write position means only a job or an outpost
/// writes this; 9 means not even they do.
///
/// The levels are not a design drawn on paper — they are the requireWorkspaceRole
/// calls the services were already making, moved to the one place that covers
/// the paths a hook does not (a job calling a service in-process, a custom
/// action nobody wired a role hook onto, a where-clause built by hand). The
/// hooks stay: a gate refuses with a level, and a person needs the sentence.
///
/// A gate is per MODEL and never per row. WHICH rows is TENANCY, declared once
/// in the `tenancy { }` block below rather than as a rule per model: every model
/// carrying `workspaceId` is scoped on it, the 14 that reach one through a
/// relation are scoped through that parent, and the FOURTEEN that legitimately
/// span workspaces say so by name with `@@tenant(none)`. They are three kinds
/// of thing and the kind is the argument:
///
///   identity          User, Credential, Session, Verification, OauthFlow,
///                     Account — a person is one person in every workspace
///   the tenant itself Workspace, and WorkspaceMember, which is what the claim
///                     is READ from
///   the installation  HubConfig and Backup describe the whole install,
///                     Blueprint/BlueprintParam are one curated catalogue
///                     every workspace sees, NotificationPreference has no
///                     workspace column at all, and OutpostNonce is a machine's
///                     spent credential, which no workspace owns and no
///                     session resolves a claim from
///
/// A NULLABLE column is not a fourth kind and `AuditEvent` used to be listed as
/// one. It is scoped like everything else, and its null means the row belongs
/// to no workspace rather than to all of them (`FJS-D141`) — sharing is a
/// property of a MODEL, said with `@@tenant(none)`, never of a row that happens
/// to have nothing in the column.
///
/// This replaced sixteen hand-written `@@allow('all', workspaceId ==
/// auth().workspaceId)` lines. The claim it grades against is resolved per
/// REQUEST — `createApp({ principal: membershipClaim(...) })` in
/// api/src/core/app.ts — because a person belongs to several workspaces and
/// holds a different role in each, which is the shape framework tenancy could
/// not express until `FJS-D113`.
///
/// A policy FILTERS, where a gate REFUSES. So the failure shape of a wrong one
/// is an empty screen and no error: any read that legitimately crosses a
/// workspace has to be asSystem() — the jobs, the hub and the outpost's
/// heartbeat all are.
///
/// ALSO ENFORCED AT THE DATA BOUNDARY, independently of any level: @guarded and
/// @encrypted. Those are field policy keyed on asSystem(), not GatePlugin, so
/// Secret.data stays unreadable and encrypted for an admin and a sysadmin alike.
///
/// CONCURRENCY — @version is on the rows a PERSON owns, and only those.
///
/// Twelve models declare it: Workspace, Project, Environment, Secret, Network,
/// Domain, FeatureFlag, NotificationChannel, AlertRule, Dashboard, Blueprint
/// (as `revision`, since `version` there is the upstream software's) and
/// HubConfig. Every one is a row a form edits. Two people on one both PATCH and the second silently
/// erases the first; with the column, the second gets a 409 it can retry off a
/// fresh read.
///
/// The rest are left out on purpose, and the reason is that @version is per ROW
/// and not per column. Where two writers touch DISJOINT columns the conflict is
/// reported against a change the person never made, and a 409 nobody can act on
/// teaches everyone to retry blindly:
///
///   Server · ApiKey · Volume        an outpost heartbeat / a usage counter
///   Deployment · Job · Recipe · App a job drives status through a run
///   DashboardWidget                 reorder writes position, updateWidget
///                                   writes config, and neither cares about
///                                   the other. Sending the whole sequence is
///                                   what makes reorder safe, not a version
///   FlagOverride                    reached by natural key, never read as a
///                                   row, so nothing in a browser holds one
///   the *Run / *Event / *Step rows  append-only, never edited twice
///
/// Deployment is the one worth stating: its concurrency guard already exists
/// and is narrower. @@transitions(status, …) is a compare-and-swap on the one
/// column two writers contend for, so a stale cancel is refused BY NAME. Adding
/// @version on top would refuse it again for a reason nobody asked about — a
/// build step having moved the row since the screen loaded it.
///
/// The database path is declared below in `database main`. litestone.config.js
/// carries the schema and migrations paths for the CLI.

// ============================================================
// DATABASES
// ============================================================
// The audit logger. Every write to a @@log(audit) model lands in
// db/audit/auditLogs.jsonl with before/after snapshots, queryable through the
// `auditLogs` accessor. Two things to know:
//   • The path is resolved relative to the PROCESS CWD, not this file — it
//     assumes the API is started from the package root.
//   • Entries flush on a ~1s timer and on exit — a read in the same session
//     immediately after a write sees nothing. That is lag, not data loss.
// @encrypted / @guarded / @secret fields are REDACTED in log entries — the
// trail records that the field was written, never what it holds. That is why
// Secret and the auth tables are logged: an access trail over a credential is
// the point, and it costs nothing to read. Requires litestone >= 2026-08-03.

// The application database. Declared here rather than passed to createClient()
// because the schema is the seed: a client that names its own file can point at
// a database this schema never described. `env()` keeps DATABASE_URL working —
// the fallback is what every local run uses.
//
// Resolved against the PROCESS CWD, like the audit path below, so both assume
// the API is started from the package root.
//
// NOTE: this declaration WINS over `createClient({ db })`. Passing both is not
// an error and produces no warning — the option is simply ignored — so core/db.ts
// deliberately does not pass one.
database main { path env("DATABASE_URL", "./db/basecamp.db") }

database audit { path env("AUDIT_PATH", "./db/audit/") driver logger retention 90d }

// ============================================================
// TENANCY
// ============================================================
//
// A workspace is the tenant, and this app is the shape the framework's own
// tenancy could not express until `FJS-D113`: a person belongs to several
// workspaces and holds a different role in each, so the claim cannot sit on
// the session — it is resolved per REQUEST, from the WorkspaceMember row, by
// `createApp({ principal: membershipClaim(...) })` in api/src/core/app.ts.
//
// Declaring it here replaces sixteen hand-written
// `@@allow('all', workspaceId == auth().workspaceId)` lines, which was sixteen
// chances to leave one off and no way to notice: a missing one is a screen
// showing another workspace's rows with a 200.
tenancy { strategy row  column workspaceId  claim workspaceId }

// ============================================================
// ENUMS
// ============================================================

enum AccountType    { individual organization }
enum WorkspaceType  { personal team enterprise }
enum WorkspaceRole  { viewer billing developer admin owner }
enum UserKind       { human bot ai }

// Whether a principal or a tenant may act at all. Two enums rather than one
// shared word, following the ServerStatus / AppStatus / JobStatus convention
// already in this file: a status is a fact about ONE model, and the sets are
// genuinely different — a workspace is never `pending_verification`.
//
// `suspended` is the value with teeth. It was a free `String` on User until
// Phase 10, which meant nothing held the column and the word it wanted to hold
// was one nothing enforced — the same shape that let AlertRule default to a
// severity its own API refused. Enforcement is core/session-auth.ts (a
// suspended user cannot sign in and their live tokens stop resolving) and
// scopeToWorkspace (a suspended workspace refuses every scoped service).
enum UserStatus      { pending_verification active suspended }
enum WorkspaceStatus { active suspended }
enum SecretKind     { ssh_key provider_key registry_auth tls_cert notification generic }
enum ServerStatus   { pending provisioning installing ready online unreachable draining stopped destroyed }
enum ServerRole     { general build database gateway worker }
enum ProviderKind   { custom hetzner }
enum EnvironmentTier { development test preview staging production }
enum AppType        { container worker database daemon cron static function }
enum AppStatus      { unknown stopped starting running stopping deploying error }
enum DeployStatus   { pending building pushing deploying success failed cancelled rolled_back }
enum StepStatus     { pending running success failed skipped }
enum JobKind        { one_shot scheduled triggered workflow }
enum JobStatus      { pending running failed cancelled }
// One run of anything a machine does on an operator's behalf — a job, a recipe
// on a server, a reclaim sweep. Three models, one set of five states, because
// three copies of the same five words drift apart one value at a time and the
// screens then disagree about what "finished" looks like.
enum RunStatus      { pending running success failed timeout }

// Alert severity. Was `String @default("medium")` on both models — a default
// the alerts service refused, because the vocabulary lived in the service and
// the schema knew nothing about it. The schema is the seed: declaring it here
// gives the column its CHECK, the API its validation and the UI its options,
// from one place (2026-08-06).
enum AlertSeverity  { info warning critical }
enum AlertSubject   { server volume }
// `support` is a session like `user` is, and is its own kind because the two
// answer different questions of the same row: with it, `actorId` is the
// OPERATOR and `onBehalfOfId` is the person they were acting as.
enum ActorKind      { user api_key system support }

// Where an alert is delivered. Each kind reads a different shape out of
// NotificationChannel.config and a different credential out of its Secret —
// which is why the kind is an enum and not a free string: the UI builds its
// form from it, and a kind nothing can deliver is a channel that silently
// never fires.
enum ChannelKind    { slack pagerduty email webhook }

// A flag is either on/off or one of several named variants. The distinction
// decides what `variants` means and what an override may pin, so it is a
// declared vocabulary rather than a boolean called `isExperiment`.
enum FlagType       { boolean variant }

// What a dashboard widget shows. A DECLARED VOCABULARY, not a stored query —
// ruled 2026-08-10, `DECISIONS.md`.
//
// A widget kind names one read this app already answers, through the service
// that owns it, so nothing is readable on a dashboard that was not readable on
// its own screen. The alternative — a widget holding `{ accessor, where }` —
// puts a read in a row and asks the server to run it for whoever opens the
// page; a row travels and a policy does not.
//
// Being an enum is what makes the picker honest: the vocabulary reaches the
// browser as a `$def` on the model's JSON Schema, the same path every other
// enum here takes, so the Add-widget list cannot offer a kind the column would
// refuse. Adding a kind is a migration, which is the cost of that.
//
// All nine read something real. What none of them can draw is a TREND:
// nothing here stores a time series, so a heartbeat's CPU is the last reading
// rather than a line, and `service_health` is a live ping with no latency
// behind it. `alert_status` has rules and events but nothing evaluating a
// threshold (`FJS-123`). Each of those three says so on the card.
enum WidgetKind {
  server_fleet
  server_health
  app_status
  deploy_feed
  job_history
  activity_feed
  alert_status
  service_health
  stat_counter
}

// How a blueprint parameter's value is minted when nobody supplies one. A
// DECLARED vocabulary rather than the mock's free string, which it parsed with
// a regex in the browser (`parseInt(generate.replace(/[^0-9]/g,''))`) — so a
// typo produced a 32-character default and said nothing. The server owns what
// each name means; the browser is told which one applies.
enum ParamGenerator { random_hex_16 random_hex_32 random_hex_64 }

// A backup is taken by hand or by the schedule. Nothing else distinguishes
// them, and the schedule's own settings live on `HubConfig`.
enum BackupKind        { manual scheduled }

// Where the archive was written. `local` is the machine basecamp runs on,
// which is a backup of a disk onto the same disk — honest, and the reason the
// screen nudges towards the second one.
enum BackupDestination { local s3 }

// What a person may be told about, and how. Seven kinds and two transports —
// `NotificationPreference` is one row per (person, kind), so a transport is a
// column rather than a third key: adding a third transport is a column, where
// a row per transport makes "off everywhere" the ABSENCE of rows and therefore
// indistinguishable from never having chosen.
//
// `deploy_failed` rather than the mock's `deploy_fail`, so it reads the same
// way as `job_failed` beside it.
enum NotificationKind {
  deploy_success
  deploy_failed
  alert_firing
  alert_resolved
  member_joined
  job_failed
  weekly_digest
}

// ============================================================
// IDENTITY — owned by @frontierjs/auth
// ============================================================
// auth.ts reaches the accessors db.user, db.credential, db.session,
// db.verification and db.oauthFlow via asSystem(), so those model names are
// load-bearing — do not rename them.
//
// `User` is the one model this app OWNS: auth ships it to be appended and
// edited, and everything below the "Basecamp additions" line is this app's.
// The other four are imported and adjusted with `extend model`; see the
// section below them.
//
// `accountId` is String, not Int. Account.id is a uuid and Int cannot hold it.
// Safe for auth.ts, which only does String(user.accountId).
//
// Everything Basecamp adds to User is nullable or defaulted ON PURPOSE:
// auth's createUser() writes only { email, name, role }, so a required
// Basecamp column would make account creation throw.

model User {
  id             String    @id @default(uuid())
  email          String    @email @unique @lower
  name           String?   @trim
  // Both carry auth's own field policy, and they carry it because this model
  // gates update at USER(4) with `@@allow('update', id == auth().id)` — your own
  // row. Without it the field policy is the only thing between a caller and
  // their own `emailVerified`, and marking your own address verified is skipping
  // the verification. Nothing here exposes a write path to `db.user` today,
  // which is exactly why it went missing from the copy unnoticed (`FJS-483`).
  //
  // `role` is auth's and is read nowhere in this app — the privileged bit is
  // `isSystemAdmin` below. It keeps the policy anyway: a column nobody reads is
  // one somebody will.
  emailVerified  Boolean   @default(false) @allow('write', auth().isAdmin)
  role           String    @default("user") @allow('write', auth().isAdmin)
  accountId      String?
  account        Account?  @relation(fields: [accountId], references: [id])

  // ── Basecamp additions ──
  //
  // `kind` and `status` are the hub's to write, so they are system-write like
  // `isSystemAdmin` below and for the same reason: basecampGateLevel() grades
  // `status == 'suspended'` STRANGER, and api-keys refuses an owner whose
  // `kind` is not `bot`. A column a caller can write is not a column a level
  // can be graded from.
  kind           UserKind   @default(human) @allow('write', auth().isSystemAdmin)
  status         UserStatus @default(pending_verification) @allow('write', auth().isSystemAdmin)
  username       String?    @trim
  displayName    String?    @trim
  avatarUrl      String?
  scopes         Json       @default([])

  // The hub tier — who may read across workspaces and suspend a tenant.
  //
  // NOT `role` above. That column is auth's, it defaults to "user", and
  // nothing in this app has ever read it; leaving the privileged bit there
  // would mean the word that grants the hub and the word auth writes are the
  // same column with two owners. The cost of a second column, accepted, is
  // that two fields next to each other look like they mean privilege — this
  // is the one this app enforces on, `role` is enforced nowhere.
  //
  // The NAME is load-bearing beyond this app: `isSystemAdmin` is what
  // sessionGateLevel() grades SYSADMIN(7) on (junction core/litestone.ts), and
  // basecampGateLevel() grades it too. core/session-auth.ts is what puts it on
  // the session.
  //
  // `@guarded` because the gate reads it: the model gates update at
  // USER(4), so without this any signed-in caller writes the column their own
  // level is computed from and lands on the hub tier. Granting it is the hub's
  // act and the hub writes through asSystem(), which passes; every read that
  // matters — session-auth building the session, /hub/users/ listing people —
  // is asSystem() for the same reason.
  isSystemAdmin  Boolean    @default(false) @allow('write', auth().isSystemAdmin)

  createdAt      DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime  @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt      DateTime?

  memberships    WorkspaceMember[]
  deployments    Deployment[]
  apiKeys        ApiKey[]

  // Read at USER(4) is auth's own ladder — an app's screens list its people.
  // Update at USER(4) is per MODEL, so on its own it says any signed-in caller
  // may rewrite any other person's row; the policy is what makes it their own
  // row. Everything this app writes to a person goes through the hub, and the
  // hub is asSystem(), which passes the policy — so nothing legitimate here
  // depends on the update path a caller reaches.
  @@gate("4.4.4.5")
  @@allow('update', id == auth().id)
  @@softDelete
  @@log(audit)
  // Both members are optional and that is the shape wanted: one username per
  // account, and no constraint at all between people who have neither.
  @@unique([accountId, username], nullsDistinct: true)
  @@index([accountId])
  @@index([email])

  // @@tenant(none) — identity spans workspaces — a person is one person in all of
  // them, and `@frontierjs/auth` owns this model
  @@tenant(none)

  // The principal `auth()` reads. Declaring it is what makes a claim GRADED:
  // without it `auth().isSystemAdmn` parses, builds, and then denies every
  // reader on the SQL side while allowing every writer on the JS side
  // (`FJS-666`). `workspaceId` is the tenancy claim and is known from the
  // `tenancy { }` block; the standing this app grades on is `isSystemAdmin`,
  // which is a column right here.
  @@auth
}

// ── The credential machinery, imported ──────────────────────────────────────
//
// Credential / Session / Verification / OauthFlow are `@frontierjs/auth`'s, and
// this app IMPORTS them rather than declaring copies. It used to declare
// copies — it had to, because each one needs three things the shipped file
// cannot know: a relation back to THIS app's User, `@@log(audit)`, and
// `@@tenant(none)`, since identity spans workspaces and row tenancy otherwise
// reports a model with no tenant column.
//
// A copy stops being the package's the first time either side moves, and
// nothing here can fail: the copies had `@guarded` on the OAuth token
// columns where the package writes `@secret`, so turning OAuth on would have
// stored every provider access and refresh token unencrypted, with this app's
// whole suite green either side of the divergence.
//
// `extend model` is how the three app-owned facts get said without owning the
// columns — litestone docs/traits.md.
import "@frontierjs/auth/schema.lite"

extend model Credential {
  user User @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  @@log(audit)
  @@tenant(none)
}

extend model Session {
  user User @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  // No @@log(audit) here — the shipped model already carries it, and a second
  // one is refused rather than merged.
  @@tenant(none)
}

extend model Verification {
  @@log(audit)
  @@tenant(none)
}

extend model OauthFlow {
  @@tenant(none)
}

// ============================================================
// ACCOUNT
// ============================================================

model Account {
  id          String      @id @default(uuid())
  type        AccountType @default(organization)
  status      String      @default("pending_verification")
  slug        String      @unique @trim @length(1, 64)
  displayName String      @trim @length(1, 200)
  avatarUrl   String?

  createdAt   DateTime    @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime    @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt   DateTime?

  users       User[]
  workspaces  Workspace[]

  @@gate("6.8")
  @@softDelete(cascade)
  @@log(audit)

  // @@tenant(none) — identity spans workspaces — a person is one person in all of
  // them, and `@frontierjs/auth` owns this model
  @@tenant(none)
}

// ============================================================
// WORKSPACE
// ============================================================

model Workspace {
  id        String        @id @default(uuid())
  accountId String
  account   Account       @relation(fields: [accountId], references: [id])

  name      String        @trim @length(1, 80)
  slug      String        @unique @trim @length(1, 64)
  type      WorkspaceType @default(team)
  ownerId   String
  settings  Json          @default("{}")

  // Suspension, which is NOT deletion. `deletedAt` means the tenant is gone
  // and @@softDelete(cascade) stamps every child with it; `suspended` means the
  // rows stay, the servers keep running and nobody in the workspace can reach
  // the API. Only the hub sets it, and scopeToWorkspace is where it bites — the
  // one hook every workspace-scoped service already runs.
  status    WorkspaceStatus @default(active)

  version   Int           @version
  createdAt DateTime      @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime      @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt DateTime?

  members     WorkspaceMember[]
  invitations Invitation[]
  projects    Project[]
  servers     Server[]
  secrets     Secret[]
  apiKeys     ApiKey[]
  jobs        Job[]
  alertRules  AlertRule[]
  channels    NotificationChannel[]
  flags       FeatureFlag[]
  auditEvents AuditEvent[]

  @@gate("1.1.5.6")
  @@softDelete(cascade)
  @@log(audit)
  @@index([accountId])

  // @@tenant(none) — the tenant itself. Scoping a workspace by the workspace claim
  // would make the switcher, /hub and the create-a-workspace path answer nothing
  @@tenant(none)
}

model WorkspaceMember {
  id          String        @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId String
  workspace   Workspace     @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  userId      String
  user        User          @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  /// This caller's authority in this workspace. Nobody may change their own.
  role        WorkspaceRole @default(viewer)
  /// What this membership may do on the models that declare @@capabilities —
  /// the grid that runs BESIDE the ladder rather than under it (`FJS-D146`).
  /// Stamped from the role at every write through `grantsFor()`; a role is the
  /// default set and not a synonym for one, so an operator may take a grant
  /// away from one person without inventing a role for them.
  ///
  /// Read onto the principal as `auth().capabilities` by `membershipClaim`,
  /// per request and per workspace — the same person holds a different set in
  /// each, which is why it is a column here and not a field on the session.
  capabilities Capability[] @default([])
  invitedBy   String?
  invitedAt   DateTime?
  acceptedAt  DateTime?

  createdAt   DateTime      @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime      @default(now()) @updatedAt

  @@gate("1.5")
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([workspaceId, userId])
  @@index([workspaceId, userId])
  // Both of the above LEAD with workspaceId, so *which workspaces is this
  // person in* — what the switcher asks on every session — had no index to use
  // (`FJS-413`). A composite is usable for a prefix and for nothing else.
  @@index([userId])

  // FJS-410. A @@gate is per MODEL, so `@@gate("1.5")` says *an admin may update
  // a membership* and has no way to say *not their own* — while `role` is the
  // column every gate in this app is graded from. `setMemberRole` is hooked
  // requireWorkspaceRole('admin', 'owner') with one guard, *cannot demote the
  // last owner*, so an admin naming their own userId with role: 'owner' was
  // granting themselves level 6. A hook is one door; a job, a hub screen and a
  // `fli tinker` session at level 5 are others.
  //
  // A model-level @@deny rather than `role @allow('write', userId != auth().id)`,
  // and the difference is the whole reason this is written twice: a FIELD write
  // policy is evaluated against the PAYLOAD (`evalJs(expr, ctx, transformed)`),
  // not against the stored row — so `userId` is undefined in a patch of
  // `{ role }`, the comparison is true, and the column is permitted. It reads
  // exactly like protection and is none. An update @@deny compiles into the
  // WHERE, against columns the row actually holds. Measured both ways in
  // db/test/schema.test.ts; the field form is `FJS-433` upstream.
  //
  // Nothing legitimately updates a caller's own membership: the invitation
  // accept CREATES the row with acceptedAt on it, leaving is a delete, and
  // asSystem() bypasses this for the hub and the setup path.
  @@deny('update', userId == auth().id)

  // What this cannot reach is the door a person walks through:
  // `workspaces.setMemberRole` writes through asSystem(), because membership
  // is what decides access and cannot be read through the caller it is
  // deciding about — and asSystem() bypasses every policy here. The refusals
  // that hold that path are in the service tier (core/hooks.ts §
  // refuseRoleAboveOwn, and the self-check in setMemberRole itself).

  // A model with no @@allow for an operation is OPEN, and litestone says so at
  // parse. With only the deny above, a scoped client read every workspace's
  // membership — measured: an authenticated caller holding no membership
  // anywhere read another workspace's row, id, user and role. Nothing in the
  // app reached it that way (every reader here is asSystem(), and the API
  // answers 404 through the workspaces service), so the boundary was open and
  // a hook was holding the door — which is Invariant 6 the wrong way round.
  //
  // Read is your own rows, across workspaces, which is what the switcher asks
  // for and why @@tenant(none) is right below. Writes are the workspace being
  // acted in: the gate already says ADMINISTRATOR(5), and this says whose
  // workspace. Neither reaches asSystem(), which is every path the app itself
  // takes.
  @@allow('read',   userId == auth().id)
  @@allow('create', workspaceId == auth().workspaceId)
  @@allow('update', workspaceId == auth().workspaceId)
  @@allow('delete', workspaceId == auth().workspaceId)

  // @@tenant(none) — this is the row the CLAIM is read from, and the workspace
  // switcher lists a person's memberships ACROSS workspaces — scoping it to the
  // current claim would answer only the workspace you are already in
  @@tenant(none)
}

// An offer of membership to an EMAIL ADDRESS — the half `addMember` cannot do.
//
// addMember takes a `userId`, so it can only reach someone who already has an
// account, and the only route that creates one is `/auth/register`, which
// leaves them with no account and no workspace. That is why this app's one door
// for a human was the setup wizard (`FJS-032`).
//
// **The row is the pending state and nothing else.** There is no `acceptedAt`
// and no `revokedAt`: accepting writes a WorkspaceMember carrying `invitedBy` /
// `invitedAt` forward and deletes this row, and revoking deletes it too. A
// lifecycle column here would mean two places record a membership's origin and
// one of them is never read. `@@log(audit)` is what survives either way.
//
// The token is the credential — whoever holds it may join. So it is
// `@guarded`: written and read through `asSystem()` only, absent from
// every scoped read the way `Session.token` is, which means the invitations
// list a screen renders cannot leak the one thing that matters. It is stored
// verbatim rather than hashed because it must be looked up BY, and a 32-byte
// random value has nothing a digest would protect it from.
model Invitation {
  id           String        @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId  String
  workspace    Workspace     @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  // `@lower` for the same reason `User.email` has it: `Ada@Example.test` and
  // `ada@example.test` are one address, and without it they are two rows under
  // `@@unique([workspaceId, email])` and two different answers to *does this
  // person already have an account*. The service lower-cases too, because it
  // compares before it writes.
  //
  // The upper bound is a real one — an address is not a place to paste a
  // document — and the lower is `@email`'s own floor plus one: the shortest
  // string litestone accepts is `a@b.c` at five, so a minimum of 5 could never
  // be violated by anything that was still an email, and 6 can. That is not
  // pedantry, it is what makes the rule testable: `verifyConstraints` builds a
  // case per bound and reports one it cannot isolate from `@email` by name
  // (`FJS-351`).
  email        String        @trim @email @lower @length(6, 200)
  role         WorkspaceRole @default(developer)

  // `global` because a token is the credential itself: it is looked up BY its
  // value, before anybody knows which workspace the invitation belongs to, so
  // it has to be unique across the installation rather than within a tenant.
  // The other 22 uniques on scoped models here are per-tenant and say so by
  // carrying `workspaceId` or a key that reaches it.
  token        String        @unique(global) @guarded
  expiresAt    DateTime

  invitedBy    String?
  createdAt    DateTime      @default(now())
  updatedAt    DateTime      @default(now()) @updatedAt

  // Read at 5 as well as written: the row names a person who is not a member
  // yet, and who has been asked to join is an administrator's business.
  @@gate("5")
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([workspaceId, email])
  @@index([workspaceId])
}

// ============================================================
// SECRETS
// ============================================================
// Machine credentials — SSH keys, provider API keys, registry auth, TLS certs.
// Renamed from `credential` because @frontierjs/auth owns that model name and
// that accessor. The distinction is real, not cosmetic: a Credential is how a
// PERSON proves identity to Basecamp; a Secret is how BASECAMP proves identity
// to a machine or a provider.
//
// VISION.md constraint 7 says Basecamp holds secrets but never shows them —
// `@guarded` is that constraint enforced at the Data boundary, so the
// value cannot be read out even by a caller allowed to list the secret.

model Secret {
  id          String     @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId String
  workspace   Workspace  @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  name        String     @trim @length(1, 200)
  kind        SecretKind @default(generic)
  data        String     @default("{}") @encrypted
  isVerified  Boolean    @default(false)
  createdBy   String?

  version     Int        @version
  createdAt   DateTime   @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime   @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt   DateTime?

  @@gate("5")
  @@softDelete
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([workspaceId, name])
}

// A token Basecamp ISSUES — the third direction, and the one the two models
// above do not cover. A Credential is how a person proves identity TO
// Basecamp; a Secret is how Basecamp proves identity to a provider; an ApiKey
// is how a machine acting FOR a person proves identity to Basecamp.
//
// The token itself is not here and is not anywhere in this app.
// @frontierjs/auth holds an HMAC of it in a Credential row and is the only
// thing that can verify one; this model owns the operational half — which
// workspace, which scopes, when it was last used, whether it is revoked — so
// that a key can be listed, audited and taken away without auth needing to
// know what a workspace is.
//
// Which means a revoked key is a row whose credential is gone, and that is
// deliberate: revoking must stop the token working immediately, and the row
// has to survive so the operator can still see what was revoked and when.
model ApiKey {
  id           String    @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId  String
  workspace    Workspace @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  // Whose permissions the key carries. A key is never more than its owner.
  userId       String
  user         User      @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  // The auth Credential holding the HMAC. A bare id, no @relation — the same
  // shape as Domain.certSecretId — because auth owns that table's lifecycle
  // and revoking DELETES the row this points at. A foreign key would either
  // block the revoke or cascade away the record of it.
  //
  // @system: derived from a token that does not exist until auth mints it, so
  // a caller has nothing to send and a generated form must not offer a box for
  // it. The application still writes it, by naming the column on the write
  // (`FJS-D22`) — which keeps the gate, the row policies and the audit actor
  // where `asSystem()` would drop all three to set one value.
  credentialId String?   @system

  name         String    @trim @length(1, 200)

  // The whole of what a list can show. Nothing in this app can reproduce the
  // token, so the mock's `reveal` button is not a feature that was skipped —
  // it is one that cannot exist without storing the token, which is the entire
  // thing an API key is designed not to do.
  //
  // @system for the same reason as credentialId, and it is the one that was
  // costing something: NOT NULL and therefore in create-mode `required`, so a
  // browser validating against the schema refused every create by naming a
  // field the caller was never meant to send (`FJS-095`).
  tokenHint    String    @system

  scopes       String[]  @default([])

  expiresAt    DateTime?
  revokedAt    DateTime?
  lastUsedAt   DateTime?
  totalUses    Int       @default(0)

  // "Uses today" is the number the screen leads with, and it needs somewhere
  // to live: a counter plus the day it counts. Deriving it would mean a row
  // per call, which is an access log — a different thing, with a different
  // retention problem.
  usageDate    String?
  usesOnDate   Int       @default(0)

  createdBy    String?
  createdAt    DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt    DateTime  @default(now()) @updatedAt

  // No @@softDelete. Revocation is this model's soft delete and it is a
  // visible state, not a hidden row; delete means the operator is done looking
  // at it. A second, invisible deleted state on top would make "revoked" and
  // "gone" four combinations instead of two.
  @@gate("5")
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([workspaceId, name])
  @@index([credentialId])
  @@index([userId])
}

// ============================================================
// SERVERS
// ============================================================

model Server {
  id               String       @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId      String
  workspace        Workspace    @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  name             String       @trim @length(1, 200)
  slug             String       @trim
  status           ServerStatus @default(pending)
  role             ServerRole   @default(general)

  // Which cloud, and the two ids that mean something only THERE — an account and
  // a machine. Not a relation: those rows live in somebody else's database, so
  // there is no key to point at and nothing to cascade. What the column can be
  // told is which clouds this app knows, and a value outside the set is refused
  // by a table CHECK rather than sitting there naming an integration nobody
  // wrote. Add a member when a provider is added.
  providerKind     ProviderKind @default(custom)
  providerId       String?
  providerServerId String?
  registerMethod   String       @default("imported")

  region           String       @default("custom")
  ipAddress        String?
  ipv6Address      String?
  sshPort          Int          @default(22)
  sshUser          String       @default("root")
  sshKeyId         String?

  outpostVersion     String?
  outpostUrl         String?
  lastHeartbeatAt  DateTime?

  plan             Json         @default("{}")
  actualSpecs      Json?
  health           Json?
  dockerState      Json?
  labels           Json         @default("{}")

  createdAt        DateTime     @default(now())
  updatedAt        DateTime     @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt        DateTime?

  events           ServerEvent[]
  volumes          Volume[]
  appServers       AppServer[]
  serverNetworks   ServerNetwork[]

  @@gate("2.4.4.5")

  // A second axis, ANDed with the gate above (`FJS-D146`). The gate answers
  // *is this caller senior enough*; this answers *is a machine theirs to touch
  // at all*, which the ladder cannot express — every developer grades USER(4)
  // and only some of them are on call.
  //
  // The derived set is create, update, delete and the three HUMAN moves:
  // `reboot`, `drain` and `undrain`. The five `@system` moves below are the
  // machine reporting on itself, and a move nobody asks for is nobody's grant,
  // so they are out of the set and out of any picker (`FJS-506`).
  //
  // Nothing here narrows what a viewer may READ — bare @@capabilities covers
  // writes and moves, and a read refusal is the silent one (`FJS-D140`).
  @@capabilities

  // WHICH servers — the tenancy block at the top of this file, not a rule
  // written here. `workspaceId` is the declared claim, resolved per request
  // from the caller's WorkspaceMember row, and compiled into the SQL of
  // every read, update and delete from a scoped client.
  //
  // The machine. Three writers move this column and each used to carry its own
  // from-list: a `transition()` helper in servers.service.ts, the provider map
  // inside `sync`, and a `transitionStates` set inside `heartbeat` — plus a
  // fourth copy in the browser deciding which buttons to draw. The move itself
  // was read-then-write in two statements, so two concurrent drains both read
  // `online`, both passed the check and both wrote; a server could be recorded
  // online while it was draining. Declared here it is a compare-and-swap on the
  // one column two writers contend for, and the loser is refused BY NAME.
  //
  // Three tiers, and the gate is what separates them:
  //   no gate   a person, at the model's own update level (developer)
  //   @gate(5)  a person one level up — taking a machine out of rotation is
  //             not the same authority as restarting it
  //   @gate(8)  the machine's own report. Nothing below asSystem() reaches it,
  //             which is what `heartbeat` runs as
  //
  // `report*` are the provider's answer relayed through a person pressing Sync,
  // so they are NOT at 8 — the write happens on that person's client and an 8
  // would refuse it. Two authorities in one move is `FJS-506`'s open question.
  //
  // What the from-lists say that the old code did not: a provider reporting
  // `running` does not undo a drain. `draining` is basecamp's word for what IT
  // is doing with the machine, and the provider has no opinion about it — the
  // old map flipped the row back to `online` silently.
  //
  // `checkIn` names the from-set heartbeat has always used. A server sitting at
  // `ready` or `provisioning` that starts checking in stays where it is; that
  // is today's behavior written down rather than a decision made here.
  // Two kinds of move, and `@system` is what says which.
  //
  // `reboot`, `drain` and `undrain` are a PERSON's: somebody decides, and the
  // gate says how senior they must be.
  //
  // The rest are the ENGINE's — an outpost checking in, a provider reporting
  // what it sees. `@system` says so: the application makes them by naming the
  // column on the write, so the gate, the row policies and the audit actor all
  // still apply, where `asSystem()` would drop all three. `checkIn` used to
  // carry `@gate(8)` for the same intent and the machine never ran, because 8
  // admits no caller at all and the heartbeat had to write the column by hand
  // against a from-set kept in the service (`FJS-506`, ruled `FJS-D150`).
  //
  // `reportRunning` and its siblings carry `@gate(5)` BESIDE `@system`, because
  // a person presses *Sync from provider* and the provider's answer decides the
  // move: the engine makes it, an administrator asks for it.
  @@transitions(status,
    reboot:           [online, unreachable]                                        -> pending,
    drain:            online                                                       -> draining @gate(5),
    undrain:          draining                                                     -> online   @gate(5),
    checkIn:          [pending, installing, unreachable]                           -> online   @system,
    reportRunning:    [pending, provisioning, installing, ready, unreachable, stopped]           -> online       @system @gate(5),
    reportStopped:    [pending, provisioning, installing, ready, online, unreachable, draining]  -> stopped      @system @gate(5),
    reportRebuilding: [pending, installing, ready, online, unreachable, draining, stopped]       -> provisioning @system @gate(5),
    reportDestroyed:  [pending, provisioning, installing, ready, online, unreachable, draining, stopped] -> destroyed @system @gate(5))
  @@softDelete
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([workspaceId, slug])
  @@index([workspaceId])
  @@index([status])
  @@index([lastHeartbeatAt])
}

model ServerEvent {
  id        String   @id @default(uuid())
  serverId  String
  server    Server   @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  kind      String
  message   String
  metadata  Json     @default("{}")
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  @@gate("2.4.8.8")
  @@index([serverId])
}

// A nonce an OUTPOST has already spent, kept exactly as long as the signature
// carrying it could still be replayed.
//
// It is a table rather than a Map because replay protection is only as good as
// the memory behind it, and a module-level Map is per PROCESS: two replicas
// each hold their own set, so a captured request replayed at the other one
// passes, and a restart forgets everything inside the freshness window
// (`FJS-376`). The signature already binds method, path, body hash and
// timestamp — this is the one part of it that needs to survive a process.
//
// `nonce` is the PRIMARY KEY and that is the mechanism, not a detail: the
// claim is the INSERT. A read-then-write is a race between the two replicas
// this exists for, and both would find the nonce absent. A duplicate key is
// refused by SQLite, atomically, and that refusal IS the replay.
//
// @@gate("8"): nothing outside asSystem() has anything to say to this table.
// @@tenant(none): a machine's credential belongs to no workspace, and the
// endpoints that spend one carry no session to resolve a claim from.
// No @@log(audit): one row per signed request would bury the human trail.
model OutpostNonce {
  nonce  String   @id
  seenAt DateTime @default(now())

  @@gate("8")
  @@tenant(none)
  @@index([seenAt])
}


// A persistent volume on a server. **Observed, not declared.**
//
// Every other model here is something a person created and Basecamp then acts
// on. A volume is the other direction: Docker made it, the outpost found it, and
// this table is Basecamp's picture of what is out there. So there is no
// `create` — a row appears because a server reported it, and disappears when a
// report no longer mentions it.
//
// Which is also why deleting a row is not deleting a volume. The volume lives
// on the server; removing the record would leave the disk exactly as full and
// the fleet one row less honest. `volumes.remove` asks the outpost, through
// Conduit, and only then forgets the row.
//
// No `workspaceId`, for the reason ServerEvent has none: a volume is
// meaningless without its server, and denormalizing the tenancy fact onto it
// would make two owners of one answer. Scope is the join — the same two
// indexed queries `servers.feed` runs.
model Volume {
  id           String    @id @default(uuid())
  serverId     String
  server       Server    @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  name         String    @trim @length(1, 255)
  driver       String    @default("local")
  mountPoint   String?

  // Bytes, not gigabytes. The outpost reports bytes, the screen decides whether
  // that reads better as MB or GB, and a rounded number stored is a number
  // nothing can un-round — `0.01 GB` in the mock is 10.7 MB.
  sizeBytes    Int       @default(0)

  // What the outpost last saw mounting it. Container NAMES, not App ids: mapping
  // a container back to an App is a guess, and a wrong join here would put a
  // volume under the wrong app on the app screen.
  inUse        Boolean   @default(false)
  containers   String[]  @default([])

  // Three different times, and they are not interchangeable. `createdOnServer`
  // is Docker's, and is the only one that answers "how long has this been
  // lying around"; `discoveredAt` is when this row appeared; `lastSeenAt` is
  // how stale the picture is, which is the difference between "no volumes" and
  // "no outpost".
  createdOnServer DateTime?
  discoveredAt    DateTime @default(now())
  lastSeenAt      DateTime @default(now())

  @@gate("2.5.5.5")
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([serverId, name])
  @@index([serverId])
}

// ============================================================
// NETWORKING
// ============================================================

model Network {
  id          String   @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId String

  name        String   @trim
  slug        String   @trim
  type        String   @default("mesh")
  cidr        String   @default("10.0.0.0/16")
  provider    String   @default("netbird")
  config      Json     @default("{}")

  version     Int      @version
  createdAt   DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt   DateTime?

  appNetworks    AppNetwork[]
  serverNetworks ServerNetwork[]

  @@gate("2.5")
  @@softDelete
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([workspaceId, slug])
}

model ServerNetwork {
  id        String   @id @default(uuid())
  serverId  String
  server    Server   @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  networkId String
  network   Network  @relation(fields: [networkId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  ipAddress String?
  joinedAt  DateTime @default(now())

  @@gate("2.5")
  @@unique([serverId, networkId])
  // networkId leads no index otherwise: the @@unique above starts with
  // serverId, and the FK cascades — SQLite scans the child table once per
  // deleted network without this (`FJS-413`).
  @@index([networkId])
}

// ============================================================
// PROJECTS
// ============================================================

model Project {
  id          String    @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId String
  workspace   Workspace @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  name        String    @trim @length(1, 80)
  slug        String    @trim @length(1, 64)
  description String?
  status      String    @default("active")
  tags        Json      @default([])
  metadata    Json      @default("{}")

  version     Int       @version
  createdAt   DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime  @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt   DateTime?

  environments Environment[]

  @@gate("2.4.4.5")
  // Scoped by the tenancy block at the top of this file. Every path that
  // legitimately crosses a workspace is asSystem() — the jobs and the hub —
  // because a policy FILTERS where a gate refuses, so a scoped read that
  // crosses answers an empty list rather than an error.
  @@softDelete(cascade)
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([workspaceId, slug])
  @@index([workspaceId])
}

model Environment {
  id          String          @id @default(uuid())
  projectId   String
  project     Project         @relation(fields: [projectId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  workspaceId String

  name        String          @trim @length(1, 64)
  slug        String          @trim @length(1, 64)
  tier        EnvironmentTier @default(development)
  isProtected Boolean         @default(false)
  /// The environment's variables — a separate grant from editing the
  /// environment itself. Renaming a staging environment and reading its
  /// secrets are the same `update` to the gate and are not the same act.
  variables   Json            @default([]) @capability

  version     Int             @version
  createdAt   DateTime        @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime        @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt   DateTime?

  apps        App[]
  deployments Deployment[]
  jobs        Job[]
  flagOverrides FlagOverride[]

  @@gate("2.4.4.5")

  // `setVariable` is one write to `Environment` and the gate has one word for
  // it, so granting somebody the ability to set a variable granted every other
  // edit to the environment with it. `@capability` on the column is the finer
  // place to bind the rule, which is what the column tier is for (`FJS-D147`).
  @@capabilities

  // Environment carries its own workspaceId rather than reaching through
  // projectId, so tenancy scopes it on a column rather than through a parent.
  @@softDelete(cascade)
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([projectId, slug])
  @@index([projectId])
}

// ============================================================
// APPS
// ============================================================
// The deployable unit inside an Environment. Named App, never Service —
// Service is the API realm's primary noun and means "the CRUD surface for a
// model". VISION.md §Vocabulary forbids the overload by name. The rest of the
// codebase already agreed: apps.service.ts, AppType, AppStatus.

model App {
  id            String      @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId   String
  environmentId String
  environment   Environment @relation(fields: [environmentId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  name          String      @trim @length(1, 80)
  slug          String      @trim @length(1, 64)
  type          AppType     @default(container)
  status        AppStatus   @default(unknown)

  source        Json        @default("{}")
  config        Json        @default("{}")
  port          Int?
  isPublic      Boolean     @default(false)

  createdAt     DateTime    @default(now())
  updatedAt     DateTime    @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt     DateTime?

  deployments   Deployment[]
  jobs          Job[]
  appServers    AppServer[]
  appNetworks   AppNetwork[]
  domains       Domain[]

  @@gate("2.4.4.5")
  // The deploy job writes App.status through asSystem(), which is how it
  // crosses the tenancy the block at the top of this file compiles in.
  @@softDelete(cascade)
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([environmentId, slug])
  @@index([workspaceId])
  @@index([environmentId])
  @@index([status])
}

// A hostname the App answers on, and the certificate that terminates it.
//
// Replaces `App.domain`, which was one nullable string and could therefore
// describe exactly one hostname with no certificate, no redirect and no
// primary. An app with an apex and a www is the ordinary case.
//
// **The certificate MATERIAL is not here.** `certSecretId` points at a
// `Secret` of kind `tls_cert`, whose `data` is `@encrypted` — so the private
// key is written once, never read back, and never lands in a column a list
// query returns. The columns here are the metadata an operator reads.
//
// `certStatus` is deliberately NOT a column: it is derived from
// `certExpiresAt` on read, because a stored status and a stored expiry are two
// owners of one fact and the stored one is the one that goes stale overnight.
model Domain {
  id            String    @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId   String
  appId         String
  app           App       @relation(fields: [appId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  hostname      String    @trim @length(1, 253)
  isPrimary     Boolean   @default(false)
  redirectTo    String?
  port          Int       @default(443)
  proxied       Boolean   @default(false)
  sslMode       String    @default("full_strict")

  certSecretId  String?
  certKind      String?
  certIssuedAt  DateTime?
  certExpiresAt DateTime?

  version       Int       @version
  createdAt     DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt     DateTime  @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt     DateTime?

  @@gate("2.5")
  @@softDelete
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([workspaceId, hostname])
  @@index([appId])
}

// Placement — which servers an App's replicas actually landed on.
// Written by the deploy job, never by a person.
model AppServer {
  id           String   @id @default(uuid())
  appId        String
  app          App      @relation(fields: [appId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  serverId     String
  server       Server   @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  replicaIndex Int      @default(0)
  status       String   @default("unknown")
  containerId  String?
  assignedAt   DateTime @default(now())
  startedAt    DateTime?
  stoppedAt    DateTime?

  @@gate("2.8")
  @@unique([appId, serverId, replicaIndex])
  @@index([serverId])
}

model AppNetwork {
  id        String   @id @default(uuid())
  appId     String
  app       App      @relation(fields: [appId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  networkId String
  network   Network  @relation(fields: [networkId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  ipAddress String?
  dnsName   String?
  joinedAt  DateTime @default(now())

  @@gate("2.8")
  @@unique([appId, networkId])
  @@index([networkId])
}

// ============================================================
// DEPLOYMENTS
// ============================================================
// A Release — a Manifest realized at a point in time (VISION.md §Where
// Basecamp Fits). The record is append-only from a user's point of view:
// creating a deployment is a USER action, advancing its status is the job's
// (SYSTEM), and erasing deployment history takes a SYSADMIN.

model Deployment {
  id                   String       @id @default(uuid())
  appId                String
  app                  App          @relation(fields: [appId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  environmentId        String?
  environment          Environment? @relation(fields: [environmentId], references: [id])
  workspaceId          String

  status               DeployStatus @default(pending)
  trigger              String       @default("manual")
  fromImage            String?
  toImage              String?
  commitSha            String?
  commitMessage        String?
  branch               String?
  author               String?
  builtImage           String?
  previousDeploymentId String?
  configSnapshot       Json         @default("{}")
  triggeredBy          String?
  triggeredByUser      User?        @relation(fields: [triggeredBy], references: [id])

  queuedAt             DateTime     @default(now())
  startedAt            DateTime?
  finishedAt           DateTime?
  durationMs           Int?

  createdAt            DateTime     @default(now())
  updatedAt            DateTime     @default(now()) @updatedAt

  steps                DeploymentStep[]

  @@gate("2.4.4.4")
  // The release pipeline, declared once and enforced at the Data boundary
  // rather than as a TERMINAL/CANCELLABLE list in the service. `succeed` comes
  // from `building` as well as the later two because the job short-circuits
  // a no-op release; `pushing` and `deploying` are written by a fuller
  // pipeline and by the seed, and the UI already tones them.
  //
  // `rollback` is @gate(5): the model's update gate is USER(4), and undoing a
  // release someone else shipped is not the same authority as shipping one.
  @@transitions(status,
    build:    pending                                 -> building,
    push:     building                                -> pushing,
    release:  pushing                                 -> deploying,
    succeed:  [building, pushing, deploying]          -> success,
    fail:     [pending, building, pushing, deploying] -> failed,
    cancel:   [pending, building, pushing, deploying] -> cancelled,
    rollback: success                                 -> rolled_back @gate(5))
  @@log(audit)
  @@index([appId])
  @@index([workspaceId])
  @@index([status])
  @@index([environmentId])
  @@index([triggeredBy])
}

model DeploymentStep {
  id           String     @id @default(uuid())
  deploymentId String
  deployment   Deployment @relation(fields: [deploymentId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  name         String
  status       StepStatus @default(pending)
  output       String?
  startedAt    DateTime?
  finishedAt   DateTime?
  durationMs   Int?

  @@gate("2.4.8.8")
  @@index([deploymentId])
}

// ============================================================
// JOBS
// ============================================================

model Job {
  id             String       @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId    String
  workspace      Workspace    @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  appId          String?
  app            App?         @relation(fields: [appId], references: [id])
  environmentId  String?
  environment    Environment? @relation(fields: [environmentId], references: [id])

  name           String       @trim @length(1, 200)
  kind           JobKind      @default(one_shot)
  status         JobStatus    @default(pending)
  command        String?
  cronExpression String?
  nextRunAt      DateTime?
  trigger        String       @default("manual")
  triggerConfig  Json         @default("{}")
  timeoutSeconds Int          @default(300)
  retryLimit     Int          @default(3)
  retryCount     Int          @default(0)
  lastRunAt      DateTime?
  lastRunStatus  String?

  createdAt      DateTime     @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime     @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt      DateTime?

  runs           JobRun[]

  @@gate("2.4.4.5")
  // A job's status is its CURRENT state, not the outcome of its last run —
  // that is `lastRunStatus`, and JobRun.status is the run's own. So a run that
  // succeeds returns the job to `pending`, which is what "idle, awaiting the
  // next fire" means here; only a failure parks it.
  //
  // `cancel` is reachable from `failed` because `remove()` cancels whatever it
  // soft-deletes: a job left `pending` behind a `deletedAt` is invisible to
  // every read and still on the clock.
  @@transitions(status,
    start:  [pending, failed]          -> running,
    idle:   running                    -> pending,
    fail:   running                    -> failed,
    cancel: [pending, running, failed] -> cancelled)
  @@softDelete
  @@log(audit)
  @@index([workspaceId])
  @@index([nextRunAt])
  @@index([appId])
  @@index([environmentId])
}

model JobRun {
  id         String       @id @default(uuid())
  jobId      String
  job        Job          @relation(fields: [jobId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  status     RunStatus    @default(pending)
  trigger    String       @default("manual")
  startedAt  DateTime     @default(now())
  finishedAt DateTime?
  durationMs Int?
  exitCode   Int?
  error      String?
  output     Json         @default("{}")

  createdAt  DateTime     @default(now())

  @@gate("2.8")
  @@index([jobId])
}

// ============================================================
// FLEET ACTIONS — recipes and reclaim
// ============================================================
// Two ways to act on a machine, and they need opposite safeguards.
//
// A RECIPE is a stored shell script. The dashboards ruling — a saved view
// names a declared KIND, never a stored query — does not transfer, and the
// reason it does not is worth stating: a stored query is dangerous because the
// Data boundary grades a CALLER against a MODEL, and a string cannot be
// graded, so the row would be run at whoever opened it. A script is not run
// here at all. It is handed to an outpost and run on a machine, where there is
// no model, no caller and no grade — it runs at whatever the outpost has, every
// time, for everyone. A vocabulary buys nothing against that.
//
// So the safeguard is the record instead. Authoring a recipe is an admin act,
// running one is attributed, and every run keeps the script it actually ran
// (`RecipeRun.script`) — because a recipe is editable, and output read against
// a script that has since changed is not evidence.
//
// A CLEANUP is the other half: it never carries a command. It names targets
// from a fixed list, so the destructive path on a machine is the one an
// operator reaches for daily and the arbitrary path is the one they need a
// role for.
//
// That list is NOT an enum here, and that is a limitation rather than a
// preference: `targets ReclaimTarget[]` does not parse — *array [] is only
// supported for Text, Integer, File, or a model name for many-to-many*. A
// declared enum plus a `String[]` column would be two homes with no CHECK
// joining them, which is exactly the shape that let `AlertRule.severity`
// default to a value its own API refused. One home instead:
// `api/src/services/cleanup/targets.ts`, which the API validates against and
// the screen fetches rather than copies.

model Recipe {
  id             String     @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId    String
  workspace      Workspace  @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  name           String     @trim @length(1, 120)
  slug           String     @trim
  description    String?    @length(0, 500)

  // The script itself. Bounded so a paste cannot become a row nothing can
  // render; the outpost's own limits are stricter than anything declarable here.
  script         String     @length(1, 20000)

  // Per recipe, not per run: how long a script may take is a property of the
  // script. The outpost is told, and the run is stamped `timeout` if it exceeds.
  timeoutSeconds Int        @default(300) @gte(5) @lte(3600)

  // Bookkeeping the list reads, written by the job. `runCount` is a counter
  // rather than a COUNT(*) because the runs it counts are pruned and the
  // number of times a script has been let loose on the fleet is not.
  lastRunAt      DateTime?
  runCount       Int        @default(0)

  createdBy      String?
  createdAt      DateTime   @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime   @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt      DateTime?

  runs           RecipeRun[]

  @@gate("4.5")
  @@softDelete
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([workspaceId, slug])
  @@index([workspaceId])
}

// One row per SERVER, not per invocation. Running a recipe across the fleet is
// N executions with N exit codes, and a single row for all of them has to pick
// one status for "three succeeded and two failed" — which is the answer an
// operator most needs and the one that shape cannot give.
model RecipeRun {
  id          String    @id @default(uuid())
  recipeId    String
  recipe      Recipe    @relation(fields: [recipeId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  serverId    String
  server      Server    @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  status      RunStatus @default(pending)

  // The script AS RUN, copied at dispatch. The recipe is editable; an output
  // read against a script that has since changed is not evidence of anything.
  script      String

  requestedBy String?
  exitCode    Int?
  stdout      String?
  stderr      String?
  error       String?

  startedAt   DateTime?
  finishedAt  DateTime?
  durationMs  Int?
  createdAt   DateTime  @default(now())

  @@gate("2.4.8.8")
  @@index([recipeId])
  @@index([serverId])
}

// What the outpost last saw about a machine's disk. OBSERVED, like Volume: a row
// exists because a server reported it, and there is no way to author one.
//
// Two things a reclaim screen wants are deliberately absent, both because
// something else already owns them:
//
//   Unused VOLUMES. `Volume` is one row per disk with its size and what mounts
//   it, so a count here would be a second answer to a question already
//   answered, and the two would disagree the first time a report was missed.
//
//   The last cleanup. `CleanupRun` records when a sweep ran and what it freed;
//   a stamp here would go stale the first time one failed.
// The columns are `docker system df`'s own: a size and a RECLAIMABLE figure per
// category, because how much of an image store can go is a question about
// shared layers that only Docker can answer. The mock multiplied a count by an
// average and printed the result as a number of gigabytes; a made-up estimate
// beside a real one is worse than no estimate, since nothing on screen says
// which is which.
//
// There is no total column. It is the three reclaimable figures plus what
// `Volume` says about unused disks, and a stored sum is a fourth number to keep
// in step with three that already move.
model DiskUsage {
  id                         String   @id @default(uuid())
  serverId                   String
  server                     Server   @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  imagesTotal                Int      @default(0)
  imagesUnused               Int      @default(0)
  imagesDangling             Int      @default(0)
  imageBytes                 Int      @default(0)
  imagesReclaimableBytes     Int      @default(0)

  containersRunning          Int      @default(0)
  containersStopped          Int      @default(0)
  containersReclaimableBytes Int      @default(0)

  buildCacheBytes            Int      @default(0)
  buildCacheReclaimableBytes Int      @default(0)

  reportedAt                 DateTime @default(now())

  @@gate("2.8")
  @@unique([serverId])
}

// A reclaim sweep on ONE server — queued, then carried out by an outpost.
//
// It is a record of what was actually freed, which is why it is a row rather
// than a toast: "we cleaned the fleet last Tuesday" is the question a disk
// filling up again asks, and nothing else here can answer it.
model CleanupRun {
  id          String    @id @default(uuid())
  serverId    String
  server      Server    @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  status      RunStatus @default(pending)

  // The vocabulary is owned by api/src/services/cleanup/targets.ts — see the
  // section note above for why it cannot be an enum here.
  targets     String[]  @default([])

  // How many images per repository survive an `unused_images` sweep. On the
  // run rather than in a setting, because it is what THIS sweep did.
  keepImages  Int       @default(3) @gte(0) @lte(50)

  requestedBy String?

  // What the outpost said it freed, never what was estimated before it ran. The
  // screen shows an estimate; only this is what happened.
  freedBytes  Int       @default(0)
  detail      Json      @default("{}")
  error       String?

  startedAt   DateTime?
  finishedAt  DateTime?
  durationMs  Int?
  createdAt   DateTime  @default(now())

  @@gate("2.4.8.8")
  @@log(audit)
  @@index([serverId])
}

// ============================================================
// FEATURE FLAGS
// ============================================================

// A flag is declared once for the workspace and OVERRIDDEN per environment.
//
// The mock keyed its per-environment state by tier name — `production`,
// `staging`, `development` — which is a vocabulary that already exists here as
// `model Environment`, one row per environment per project. Keying by the
// string would have meant every project in the workspace sharing one
// "production" that belongs to none of them, and no way to say "on in THIS
// project's staging". `FlagOverride` points at the real row.
//
// The flag row therefore carries the DEFAULT — what an environment with no
// override gets — and nothing else about rollout lives on it.
model FeatureFlag {
  id          String    @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId String
  workspace   Workspace @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  // The name an SDK asks for. Slug-shaped because it is an identifier in
  // somebody else's source code, not a title: `new-checkout-flow`.
  key         String    @trim @slug @length(1, 120)
  description String?
  type        FlagType  @default(boolean)
  tags        String[]  @default([])

  // Only meaningful for type=variant: [{ key, name, weight }]. Left as Json
  // rather than a model because a variant has no identity outside its flag —
  // nothing references one, and the whole set is rewritten as a unit.
  variants    Json      @default([])

  isEnabled   Boolean   @default(false)
  // 100, not 0. `isEnabled` is the switch and this NARROWS it, so the pair a
  // new flag starts with has to mean "off, and when you turn it on, everyone".
  // Defaulting to 0 makes enabling a flag do nothing for anybody the moment an
  // SDK starts bucketing, with the screen showing it on (FJS-124). Rolling out
  // to nobody is `isEnabled: false`, which is the state that already says it.
  rollout     Int       @default(100) @gte(0) @lte(100)

  createdBy   String?
  version     Int       @version
  createdAt   DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime  @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt   DateTime?

  overrides   FlagOverride[]

  @@gate("2.4.4.5")
  @@softDelete
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([workspaceId, key])
  @@index([workspaceId])
}

model FlagOverride {
  id            String      @id @default(uuid())
  flagId        String
  flag          FeatureFlag @relation(fields: [flagId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  environmentId String
  environment   Environment @relation(fields: [environmentId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  isEnabled     Boolean     @default(false)
  rollout       Int         @default(100) @gte(0) @lte(100)   // see FeatureFlag.rollout
  // Which variant this environment is pinned to, for a type=variant flag. Null
  // means "weighted as declared" rather than "off".
  variantKey    String?

  createdAt     DateTime    @default(now())
  updatedAt     DateTime    @default(now()) @updatedAt

  @@gate("2.4.4.4")
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([flagId, environmentId])
  @@index([environmentId])
}

// ============================================================
// ALERTS
// ============================================================

// Where alerts are delivered — Slack, PagerDuty, email, a webhook.
//
// Two things are deliberately NOT columns here:
//
//   The credential. A Slack webhook URL and a PagerDuty integration key are
//   both bearer credentials — anyone holding one can post as the workspace —
//   so they live in a `Secret` (`@encrypted`, written once, never read back)
//   and this model keeps only the reference. Same ruling as Domain's
//   certificate material. `config` therefore holds routing only: the channel
//   override, the recipient list, the HTTP method. Nothing in it is a secret,
//   which is why the whole column comes back on a read.
//
//   How many alerts went out today. The mock shows a per-channel count, and a
//   counter on this row would be a second owner of a fact that belongs to the
//   deliveries — which do not exist yet, because delivery needs an outbound
//   adapter this app does not have. `lastTestAt` and `lastDeliveryAt` are
//   stamps of things that actually happened; a count with no rows behind it
//   would be a number nothing could reconcile.
model NotificationChannel {
  id          String      @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId String
  workspace   Workspace   @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  name        String      @trim @length(1, 200)
  kind        ChannelKind
  config      Json        @default("{}")

  // Bare id, no @relation — the same shape as Domain.certSecretId. Secret is
  // @@softDelete, so the row a channel points at is never actually removed,
  // and a channel whose secret was retired should say so rather than vanish
  // with it.
  secretId    String?

  // The plaintext credential, on the wire and nowhere else: the service lifts
  // it into a Secret row (@encrypted) and keeps `secretId`. @transient is what
  // says so — no column, no audit entry, never in a response — and declaring it
  // is what makes the API's own form offer a box for it and a typo distinct
  // from a field the model does not have (`FJS-D23`).
  secret      String?     @transient @length(1, 4096) @label("Credential")

  isActive    Boolean     @default(true)
  lastTestAt      DateTime?
  lastDeliveryAt  DateTime?
  createdBy   String?

  version     Int         @version
  createdAt   DateTime    @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime    @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt   DateTime?

  rules       AlertRuleChannel[]

  @@gate("2.5")
  @@softDelete
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([workspaceId, name])
  @@index([workspaceId])
}

model AlertRule {
  id          String    @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId String
  workspace   Workspace @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  name        String    @trim @length(1, 200)
  description String?
  severity    AlertSeverity @default(warning)
  metricName  String
  condition   Json      @default("{}")
  isActive    Boolean   @default(true)

  version     Int       @version
  createdAt   DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime  @default(now()) @updatedAt

  events      AlertEvent[]
  channels    AlertRuleChannel[]

  @@gate("2.5")
  @@log(audit)
  @@index([workspaceId])
}

// Which channels a rule delivers to.
//
// Was `AlertRule.channels Json @default("[]")` — an array of ids pointing at
// rows that did not exist, because NotificationChannel did not exist. A foreign
// key with no constraint and no reader: nothing could tell a live channel id
// from a typo, and deleting a channel left every rule holding it silently
// pointing at nothing. The join is the constraint.
model AlertRuleChannel {
  id        String              @id @default(uuid())
  ruleId    String
  rule      AlertRule           @relation(fields: [ruleId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  channelId String
  channel   NotificationChannel @relation(fields: [channelId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  createdAt DateTime            @default(now())

  @@gate("2.5")
  @@unique([ruleId, channelId])
  @@index([channelId])
}

// Fired by the system; acknowledged by a person — hence the split gate.
model AlertEvent {
  id             String     @id @default(uuid())
  ruleId         String
  rule           AlertRule  @relation(fields: [ruleId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  status         String        @default("firing")
  severity       AlertSeverity
  // What fired — the pair carries no foreign key, so a destroyed server leaves
  // its history readable instead of taking it away, which is what an event log
  // is for. The type is an enum so the engine that writes these cannot invent a
  // subject nothing measures.
  subjectType    AlertSubject
  subjectId      String
  valueAtTrigger Float      @default(0)
  message        String
  firedAt        DateTime   @default(now())
  resolvedAt     DateTime?
  acknowledgedBy String?
  acknowledgedAt DateTime?

  @@gate("2.8.4.8")
  @@index([ruleId])
  @@index([status])
}

// ============================================================
// DASHBOARDS
// ============================================================
// A dashboard is a saved arrangement of widgets over data this app already
// has. Two things are deliberately NOT here:
//
//   A query. A widget names a KIND, and the kind decides what is read — see
//   WidgetKind above. Storing a query would put the read in a row: the row
//   travels (it is seeded, exported, shared across a workspace) and the policy
//   does not, so whoever opened the dashboard would run a query nobody graded.
//   @@gate and @@allow grade a caller against a model, never against a string.
//
//   A color. The mock stores a hex per dashboard; @frontierjs/css styles by
//   tone, and a stored color is one the theme cannot follow. The icon is an
//   emoji, which is content rather than styling.

model Dashboard {
  id          String    @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId String
  workspace   Workspace @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  name        String    @trim @length(1, 120)
  slug        String    @trim
  description String?

  // An emoji, so it is stored as text with a length bound rather than as a
  // name from an icon set this app does not ship.
  icon        String?   @length(0, 8)

  // The WORKSPACE's pin, not the reader's. A per-person pin needs a per-person
  // preferences store, which is the model UserSettingsView is waiting on; one
  // boolean here would be a shared switch that reads as a personal one.
  isPinned    Boolean   @default(false)

  createdBy   String?
  version     Int       @version
  createdAt   DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime  @default(now()) @updatedAt
  deletedAt   DateTime?

  widgets     DashboardWidget[]

  @@gate("2.4.4.4")
  @@softDelete
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([workspaceId, slug])
  @@index([workspaceId])
}

model DashboardWidget {
  id          String     @id @default(uuid())
  dashboardId String
  dashboard   Dashboard  @relation(fields: [dashboardId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  kind        WidgetKind

  // The subject, as a real relation rather than an id inside `config`. Same
  // ruling AlertRule.channels got: a Json array pointing at rows no model
  // declares is a reference nothing can follow and nothing can clean up.
  // Which subject a kind takes — and whether it is required — is declared once
  // in api/src/services/dashboards/kinds.ts, and a data test holds the two
  // together.
  //
  // SetNull rather than Cascade: a widget whose server is gone is a widget
  // that says so, not a widget that disappears with the machine. It is only
  // the hard-delete path — Server and App are @@softDelete, so the common case
  // is a row still present and excluded from every read, which the screen
  // resolves the same way.
  serverId    String?
  server      Server?    @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  appId       String?
  app         App?       @relation(fields: [appId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  // Knobs, never a query: a portal service id, a stat_counter's source and
  // label. The keys each kind accepts are declared in kinds.ts and everything
  // else is rejected at the boundary.
  config      Json       @default("{}")

  // Thirds of a row, which is the grid the detail screen lays out. Bounded
  // here so the column cannot hold a width no layout has.
  cols        Int        @default(1) @gte(1) @lte(3)
  position    Int        @default(0)

  createdAt   DateTime   @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime   @default(now()) @updatedAt

  @@gate("2.4.4.4")
  @@log(audit)
  @@index([dashboardId])
  @@index([serverId])
  @@index([appId])
}

// ============================================================
// AUDIT
// ============================================================
// "An audit trail — every operational action attributable to a person"
// (VISION.md §Operate). Written by the system, never edited, never deleted:
// update and delete are LOCKED, which not even asSystem() passes.

model AuditEvent {
  id          String     @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId String?
  workspace   Workspace? @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  actorId     String?
  // WHO acted. Four kinds and `hooks.ts` writes all four, so this is a closed
  // set and says so.
  actorType   ActorKind  @default(user)
  /// SUPPORT MODE — who the operator was acting AS.
  ///
  /// `actorId` stays the person who really did it, which is the opposite of
  /// what every hand-rolled impersonation records and is the whole complaint in
  /// `FJS-142`. Null outside an episode.
  ///
  /// NOT `subjectId`, which on this model is the ROW that was acted on. The
  /// framework's own trail uses `subjectId` for the PERSON, in the sense a data
  /// subject is a person; this table already spent the word, so it uses another.
  onBehalfOfId String?
  action      String
  // WHAT was acted on, written as the SERVICE name (`hooks.ts`). Deliberately a
  // String and not an enum: the set grows with every service, so an enum here
  // would refuse the first row a new service writes — which is the one case
  // `fli check`'s `polymorphic-subject` exempts, and why this app baselines it.
  subjectType String
  subjectId   String
  diff        Json?
  createdAt   DateTime   @default(now())

  @@gate("5.8.9.9")
  @@index([workspaceId])
  @@index([subjectType, subjectId])

  // SCOPED, by declaring nothing: the `tenancy { }` block sees `workspaceId`
  // and this model takes the ordinary desugar. It said `@@tenant(none)` on the
  // argument that a null column never equals a claim, so scoping it would hide
  // the rows with no workspace rather than sharing them — which is true, and is
  // the intended reading rather than the cost of it. A null here says the row
  // belongs to NO workspace and the hub alone may read it (`FJS-D141`); the
  // rows that hold one are the workspace's own trail, at the Data boundary
  // rather than in the one service that remembers to filter.
}

// ============================================================
// CATALOGUE
// ============================================================
// What can be deployed, and what has been pushed to the registry. Both are
// things an operator PICKS from rather than authors, and they sit at opposite
// ends of the same question: a blueprint is a template nobody has run yet, a
// registry image is an artefact somebody has already built.

// A pre-configured deployment template — image, ports, storage and the
// parameters a person fills in before it becomes an App.
//
// **Hub-curated, so it carries no workspaceId and says `@@tenant(none)`.** The
// mock's marketplace is a catalogue of third-party software (n8n, Postgres,
// Redis, Meilisearch, Plausible); not one of its entries is somebody's own.
// Making it workspace-scoped would give every tenant a private copy of the same
// nine rows, and the alternative — a nullable `workspaceId` meaning *shared* —
// is refused outright: a null means the row belongs to NO workspace and only
// the hub reads it, which is the opposite of shared (`FJS-D141`). A workspace
// authoring its OWN blueprint is therefore a second model, not a nullable
// column on this one, and it is not owed until somebody asks for it.
//
// The `app` block in the mock's JSON is flattened into columns here, and the
// names are `App`'s own where they overlap, so composing one into the other is
// a copy rather than a translation.
model Blueprint {
  id             String     @id @default(uuid())
  slug           String     @trim @length(1, 64) @unique
  name           String     @trim @length(1, 80)

  // A FREE STRING, and deliberately not an enum, which is a departure from
  // `WidgetKind` and `ChannelKind` beside it. Those are enums because each
  // value names a read or a delivery the server implements, so a value the
  // column would refuse is a feature that does not exist. A category names
  // nothing: it is a filter chip, and the list of them is `distinct(category)`
  // over the rows. Making it an enum would turn *add a blueprint in a new
  // category* into a migration for no gain.
  category       String     @trim @length(1, 40)

  description    String     @trim @length(1, 300)
  version        String     @trim @length(1, 40)
  image          String     @trim @length(1, 200)

  // Presentational, and the one place this schema stores a color. It is the
  // vendor's brand, which is data about a third party rather than a styling
  // decision — a tone (`danger`, `info`) cannot say *n8n pink*. The UI uses it
  // for the card's icon tile and nothing else; every other surface on the card
  // is `@frontierjs/css`'s.
  icon           String?    @trim @length(1, 8)
  brandColor     String?    @trim @length(4, 9)

  // The App this blueprint becomes. Column for column what `App` and its
  // deployment config already hold.
  appType        AppType    @default(container)
  port           Int?       @gte(1) @lte(65535)
  persistent     Boolean    @default(false)
  volumePath     String?    @trim @length(1, 255)
  healthCheck    String?    @trim @length(1, 255)
  replicas       Int        @default(1) @gte(1) @lte(50)
  cpuLimit       String?    @trim @length(1, 16)
  memLimit       String?    @trim @length(1, 16)

  // Operator-facing prose shown once, on the deploy modal.
  notes          String?    @length(0, 1000)

  // `[{ label, url }]`. Json rather than a child model because these point at
  // documentation on the open web — nothing joins them, nothing queries them,
  // and no row anywhere refers to one. That is the distinction `AlertRule`
  // taught: its `channels` was a Json array naming rows no model declared, and
  // became a real join. A link names nothing here.
  links          Json       @default([])

  // A blueprint that is no longer offered but is still referenced by the apps
  // it created. Withdrawn rather than deleted, for the same reason `Recipe`
  // soft-deletes: the record of what something was built from outlives the
  // offer to build it again.
  deprecatedAt   DateTime?

  createdAt      DateTime   @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime   @default(now()) @updatedAt

  // `revision`, not `version` — `version` above is the upstream software's
  // (`1.1.1`, `16`, `7.2`) and is what the card shows. This one is the
  // optimistic-concurrency column two editors race on, and `@version` marks
  // whichever column carries it rather than requiring a name.
  revision       Int        @version

  params         BlueprintParam[]

  // Read by anyone signed in — VISITOR(1), because browsing the catalogue is
  // what a person with no workspace yet is doing. Written by the hub tier only.
  @@gate("1.7")
  @@log(audit)
  @@index([category])

  // @@tenant(none) — a curated catalogue is the same nine rows for every
  // workspace, and there is no column to scope it by
  @@tenant(none)
}

// One field a person fills in before a blueprint becomes an App — which is an
// environment variable, with a label and a rule about who mints it.
//
// A child model rather than a Json array on `Blueprint`, because these are
// ORDERED, individually validated, and the deploy form is generated from them:
// a Json array would put the shape of a form in a column nothing describes,
// which is the one place this repo has been bitten twice.
model BlueprintParam {
  id           String          @id @default(uuid())
  blueprintId  String
  blueprint    Blueprint       @relation(fields: [blueprintId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  // The environment variable itself — `N8N_ENCRYPTION_KEY`, not a display name.
  key          String          @trim @length(1, 100)
  label        String          @trim @length(1, 80)
  hint         String?         @trim @length(0, 200)

  // NOT `@secret`, and the difference matters: this is the value shipped with
  // the catalogue (`admin`, `UTC`, `256mb`), never anybody's. What a person
  // types lands in a `Secret` or in the app's own env, where `secret` below
  // says which.
  defaultValue String?         @length(0, 500)

  required     Boolean         @default(false)
  // Whether the VALUE a person supplies is a credential — the form masks it and
  // the deploy writes it as a secret env var rather than a plain one.
  secret       Boolean         @default(false)
  // Absent means the person supplies it. Present means the form offers a minted
  // value they can regenerate.
  generate     ParamGenerator?

  // The order the form asks in. Explicit, because a blueprint's parameters have
  // an order its author chose and `createdAt` is not it.
  position     Int             @default(0)

  @@gate("1.7")
  @@unique([blueprintId, key])
  @@index([blueprintId])

  // @@tenant(none) — it belongs to a Blueprint, which spans workspaces
  @@tenant(none)
}

// One tag in a container registry. OBSERVED, like `Volume` and `DiskUsage`: a
// row exists because a registry reported it, and there is no way to author one.
//
// **This commits to MIRRORING a registry rather than querying it live**, which
// is the choice `SCREENS.md` left open for `RegistryView`. Mirroring is what
// makes `inUse` answerable at all — it is a join against this workspace's
// deployments, and a live registry API knows nothing about who deployed what.
// The cost is staleness, which `observedAt` is here to make visible rather than
// hide: a repository nobody has synced reads as empty, and the screen can tell
// that from a repository with no tags.
//
// There is no `RegistryRepo` model. A repository is `distinct(repository)` over
// these rows plus a `SUM(sizeBytes)`, and a stored total is a second answer to
// a question the rows already answer — the same reasoning that keeps a total
// off `DiskUsage`.
model RegistryImage {
  id          String    @id @default(uuid())
  workspaceId String
  workspace   Workspace @relation(fields: [workspaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  repository  String    @trim @length(1, 200)
  tag         String    @trim @length(1, 128)

  // Two tags of one digest is the ordinary case (`v2.14.1` and `latest`), so
  // this is NOT unique — the unique key is the tag, which is what a registry
  // addresses and what a delete removes.
  digest      String    @trim @length(1, 100)

  // Bytes, for `Volume`'s reason: the registry reports bytes and a stored
  // megabyte is a number nothing can un-round.
  sizeBytes   Int       @default(0)

  // Whether any live deployment addresses this tag. Derived at sync and stored,
  // because the screen's whole purpose is deciding what is safe to delete and
  // recomputing it per row per render is a join per row.
  inUse       Boolean   @default(false)

  pushedAt    DateTime?
  pushedBy    String?   @trim @length(0, 120)

  // When the mirror last saw it — the difference between *no images* and *no
  // sync*, which is `Volume.lastSeenAt`'s distinction.
  observedAt  DateTime  @default(now())

  // Read by a viewer; written only by the sync. Deleting a TAG is an
  // administrator's action and really removes it at the registry, which is why
  // delete is 5 and not 8.
  @@gate("2.8.8.5")
  @@log(audit)
  @@unique([workspaceId, repository, tag])
  @@index([workspaceId])
  @@index([workspaceId, repository])
}

// ============================================================
// HUB OPERATIONS
// ============================================================
// The tier above every tenant — what `/hub/` reads and writes. Both models here
// are `@@tenant(none)` because they describe the INSTALLATION, which no
// workspace owns.

// One archive of the application database and its configuration.
//
// The outcome columns are written at SYSTEM, exactly as `RecipeRun` and
// `CleanupRun` are: a person asks for a backup and a machine reports what it
// managed, and a status a caller can write is not evidence of anything.
// `RunStatus` is reused rather than restated — its own comment says why three
// models share five states, and this is the fourth.
model Backup {
  id           String            @id @default(uuid())

  kind         BackupKind        @default(manual)
  status       RunStatus         @default(pending)
  destination  BackupDestination @default(local)

  // Absent until it finishes. Bytes.
  sizeBytes    Int?

  // Where the archive landed — a path for `local`, an object key for `s3`.
  // Never a URL with a credential in it.
  location     String?           @trim @length(0, 500)
  error        String?           @length(0, 2000)

  // Who asked. Null for the scheduled one, which is the app acting on its own
  // behalf and has no actor by construction.
  requestedBy  String?

  startedAt    DateTime?
  finishedAt   DateTime?
  durationMs   Int?
  createdAt    DateTime          @default(now())

  // A sysadmin reads them and asks for one; the job writes the outcome;
  // pruning an old archive is a sysadmin's again.
  @@gate("7.7.8.7")
  @@log(audit)
  @@index([status])
  @@index([createdAt])

  // @@tenant(none) — a backup is of the whole installation, every workspace in it
  @@tenant(none)
}

// The installation's own settings. ONE ROW, keyed by a constant.
//
// A singleton with typed columns rather than a key/value table, because a
// key/value table is a schema this file does not describe: no validation, no
// JSON Schema, no generated form, and a typo in a key is a setting that
// silently reverts to its default. The cost is that adding a setting is a
// migration, which is the correct cost for something the app's behavior reads.
//
// **The credentials in the mock's Settings screen are deliberately absent** —
// its auth secret, agent secret, Resend key and Infisical token. Three reasons,
// and each on its own is enough: they are read at BOOT, before any database, so
// a row here would not reach the running process; rotating one is a deploy, not
// a form; and this app already has a model for credential material (`Secret`,
// whose `data` is `@encrypted` and unreadable at every level). What is here is
// configuration a person can genuinely change while the app runs.
model HubConfig {
  // Not a uuid. There is one row and its id is the constant every reader asks
  // for, so a second row is a mistake with an obvious symptom rather than a
  // silent fork where two screens disagree about what the installation is
  // called.
  id                        String   @id @default("hub")

  name                      String   @trim @length(1, 80) @default("Basecamp")

  // Used for webhook callbacks, outpost registration and every link in an
  // email — so it is the one setting that is wrong in a way nothing local can
  // detect: mail goes out with a hostname nobody can reach.
  //
  // `@url` and no `@length`. A bound alongside it cannot be ISOLATED — every
  // 301-character string that would exercise it is refused by `@url` first, so
  // `verifyConstraints` reports the length rule as unchecked rather than
  // passing it on the wrong refusal (`FJS-351`). A rule nothing can test is a
  // rule nobody should declare.
  baseUrl                   String   @trim @url
  adminEmail                String   @email @lower

  // A server goes `unreachable` after this long without a check-in. Bounded
  // below by the outpost's own heartbeat interval — anything under it marks
  // every healthy machine unreachable between beats.
  heartbeatTimeoutSeconds   Int      @default(120) @gte(30) @lte(3600)

  // Hours. The default is seven days, which is what the mock showed as 168.
  sessionTtlHours           Int      @default(168) @gte(1) @lte(8760)

  requireTwoFactorForOwners Boolean  @default(false)
  allowApiKeyAuth           Boolean  @default(true)
  allowBotUsers             Boolean  @default(true)

  // The scheduled backup. The cron lives here rather than on `Backup` because
  // it describes the schedule, and a `Backup` row is one thing that already
  // happened. A schedule that comes from a ROW has to be re-registered at boot
  // and unregistered when it stops being true — `app.jobs.unschedule`.
  backupEnabled             Boolean  @default(false)
  backupCron                String   @default("0 2 * * *") @trim @length(1, 100)
  backupDestination         BackupDestination @default(local)

  mailFromAddress           String?  @email @lower
  mailFromName              String?  @trim @length(0, 80)

  updatedAt                 DateTime @default(now()) @updatedAt
  version                   Int      @version

  // Nothing below the hub tier reads or writes this — it is the installation,
  // not a tenant's.
  @@gate("7")
  @@log(audit)

  // @@tenant(none) — one row for the whole installation
  @@tenant(none)
}

// ============================================================
// PREFERENCES
// ============================================================

// What one person wants to be told about, and how.
//
// One row per (person, kind), with a column per transport. Per PERSON and not
// per membership: the mock asks once, and a preference that had to be set again
// in every workspace is a preference nobody sets. If that turns out to be
// wrong, the fix is a row per (person, workspace, kind) — and NOT a nullable
// `workspaceId` meaning *everywhere*, because a null says the row belongs to no
// workspace and hides it from every one of them (`FJS-D141`).
//
// **The absence of a row is not *off*.** A person who has never opened this
// screen has no rows, and the service resolves an unset kind to the kind's own
// default rather than to silence — which is why this model can be empty for
// everyone and the app still delivers.
model NotificationPreference {
  id       String           @id @default(uuid())
  userId   String
  user     User             @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  kind     NotificationKind
  email    Boolean          @default(false)
  inApp    Boolean          @default(true)

  updatedAt DateTime        @default(now()) @updatedAt

  // VISITOR(1) — this is your own row and belongs to no workspace, so standing
  // in one has nothing to say about it. The policy is what confines it, and it
  // names `userId` on create rather than defaulting the column: a create policy
  // is checked BEFORE a default is stamped, so `@default(auth().id)` here would
  // grade an absent column and admit everybody.
  @@gate("1")
  @@allow('all', userId == auth().id)
  @@unique([userId, kind])
  @@index([userId])

  // @@tenant(none) — a person is one person in every workspace, and this row
  // has no workspace column to scope by
  @@tenant(none)
}
